[{:registry/operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit], :actor/wave 3, :registry/required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger], :actor/scope-excluded-ops [:finalize-breeding-decision :commit-to-breeding-decision :determine-animal-fitness-for-breeding :decide-animal-welfare-disposition :authorize-veterinary-treatment :order-veterinary-treatment :override-farm-safety-officer-judgment :override-worker-safety-judgment :override-worker-animal-handling-judgment], :actor/robotics? true, :adr/related ["adr-2607121000-cloud-itonami-global-isic-isco-reverse-toposort-plan" "adr-2607152500-cloud-itonami-wave4-rollout-start-amendment" "adr-2799009332-cloud-itonami-isco-9332-animal-drawn-vehicle-drivers-coverage" "adr-2799007111-cloud-itonami-isco-7111-house-builders-coverage"], :adr/status :accepted, :actor/scope-excluded-terms ["finalized the breeding decision" "finalize the breeding decision" "committed to the breeding decision" "commit to the breeding decision" "determined the animal's fitness for breeding" "determine the animal's fitness for breeding" "decided the animal's fitness for breeding" "decide the animal's fitness for breeding" "decided the animal's welfare disposition" "decide the animal's welfare disposition" "authorized the veterinary treatment" "authorize the veterinary treatment" "ordered the veterinary treatment" "order the veterinary treatment" "administered veterinary treatment" "administer veterinary treatment" "overrode the farm safety officer's judgment" "override the farm safety officer's judgment" "overrode the worker's safety judgment" "override the worker's safety judgment" "overrode the worker's animal-handling judgment" "override the worker's animal-handling judgment" "動物の繁殖適性を判定した" "動物の福祉処分を決定した" "農場安全担当者の判断を上書きした" "作業者の安全判断を上書きした" "作業者の動物取扱判断を上書きした" "獣医治療を指示した" "繁殖決定を確定した"], :adr/id 2799006129, :phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record :flag-welfare-concern}, :auto #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}}}", :actor/domain :agriculture/animal-producer-farm-scheduling-and-logistics-coordination, :actor/proposal-ops [:log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-welfare-concern :coordinate-supply-order], :actor/hard-checks [:no-actuation :op-not-allowed :unknown-worker :worker-unverified :missing-farm-id :unknown-farm :farm-unverified :farm-mismatch :work-record-decision-forbidden :crew-schedule-override-forbidden :scope-excluded], :registry/maturity :implemented, :actor/isco-08 "6129", :actor/always-escalate-ops [:flag-welfare-concern], :adr/doc_type "adr", :operation/nodes [:intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold], :operation/entry-point :intake, :adr/body "# ADR 2799006129: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 6129 Animal Producers Not Elsewhere Classified farm scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 6129\n-- Animal Producers Not Elsewhere Classified** only, part of the\nWave3 physical-production rollout (`kotoba.occupation.wave`\nsub-major \"61\").\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"6129\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"6129\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Animal Producers Not Elsewhere\nClassified\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR\nthe entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies\n[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no\nrepository existed at `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6129` (`gh api`\n404 confirmed before scaffolding, and again immediately before repo\ncreation). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the `6129`\nentry to `:implemented`.\n\n**This actor carries a dual safety dimension: BOTH a physical-safety\ndimension (animal-handling injury risk to workers) AND an animal-\nwelfare dimension (the animal's treatment/breeding/working\nconditions).** Animal Producers NEC (livestock/farm-animal breeders\nnot covered by a more specific ISCO-08 class) work directly with\nanimals, so a wrongful automated decision could compound harm across\ntwo axes at once -- an injury to a worker, or a welfare/breeding\nmisjudgment harming the animal. The controlling precedent is\n**ADR-2607152500** (Wave4 rollout start amendment), which established\nthat decisions directly touching safety, dignity or subsistence must\nalways exclude the closed op allowlist and always escalate to a\nhuman, not merely be gated behind a confidence threshold. This ADR\napplies that guardrail shape to both axes simultaneously: no op\nanywhere in this actor's closed allowlist may finalize an animal-\ntreatment/welfare/breeding decision, or override a farm safety\nofficer's judgment.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and for the\ndual-dimension safety-guardrail pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-9332`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-9332)\n(Drivers of Animal-drawn Vehicles and Machinery: `store`/`advisor`/\n`governor`/`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set,\nalready-landed sibling with the correct animal-welfare guardrail\npattern), and\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111)\n(House Builders, standard module shape), both cloned and read in full\nthis session. The closed-allowlist + registered-AND-verified\nprovenance + content-based scope-exclusion SAFETY PATTERN follows the\nsame shape already established by this fleet's other safety-critical\noccupation actors, adapted to this vertical's animal-treatment/\nwelfare/breeding guardrail rather than the road-safety/animal-welfare\ndual-axis of 9332 (this actor has no vehicle-operation or traffic-\nnavigation surface at all -- it is farm scheduling/logistics only).\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6129` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for animal producers not\nelsewhere classified, following the SAME governed-actor architecture\nas every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Farm\nScheduling Advisor ⊣ HusbandryGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned\nidentically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- this is a different\nlibrary coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated\n`20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **farm scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never direct animal-treatment/welfare/breeding authority** -- it\nmust NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising ANY of: an animal-treatment/welfare/breeding decision, or\nan override of a farm safety officer's or worker's safety judgment.\nThese are not merely gated behind escalation -- they are structurally\nabsent from the op-allowlist entirely, and enforced a second,\nindependent way via a content-based scope-exclusion check over free\ntext.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- feeding-schedule/animal-condition-check-in\n  data logging (metadata only -- checkin id, feeding-schedule status,\n  animal condition check-in, timestamp -- NEVER a treatment decision)\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task scheduling proposal (advance\n  scheduling logistics only -- never a live animal-handling override\n  or a real-time farm-safety-officer-judgment override)\n- `:flag-welfare-concern` -- surface an animal-welfare/injury-risk\n  concern, ALWAYS escalates immediately, NEVER auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- feed/supplies procurement proposal,\n  escalates above a registered per-farm cost threshold\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles**:\nan animal-treatment/welfare/breeding decision, or an override of a\nfarm safety officer's or worker's safety judgment. These are\n**structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely**, not merely\ngated behind escalation -- an op such as `:finalize-breeding-decision`\nor `:determine-animal-fitness-for-breeding` cannot even be proposed by\nthe reference mock advisor (its `infer` function only ever emits one\nof the four allowlist ops, driven by a `case` expression with no\ndefault branch producing any other op shape), and if any code path (a\nforged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever\nproduced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check\n(`:op-not-allowed`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently.\nVerified two ways: (a) unit tests directly against `governor/check`\nfor eight concretely-named forbidden op fixtures\n(`husbandry.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-breeding-decision`, `:commit-to-breeding-decision`,\n`:determine-animal-fitness-for-breeding`,\n`:decide-animal-welfare-disposition`,\n`:authorize-veterinary-treatment`, `:order-veterinary-treatment`,\n`:override-farm-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:override-worker-safety-judgment`,\n`:override-worker-animal-handling-judgment`), and (b) an END-TO-END\ntest that drives each of those same ops through a ROGUE advisor and\nthe FULL `langgraph.graph` actor graph (not just the governor's pure\ncheck function), proving the StateGraph itself always resolves to\n`:hold` with zero records committed even when the advisor node itself\nis compromised\n(`husbandry.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`).\n\n### Seven HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Worker unregistered/unverified** -- any proposal must resolve to\n   an independently `:verified?` worker/farm-hand record. Never trusts\n   the proposal's own claim of who the worker is. Directly implements\n   the task's \"worker/farm record must be independently verified/\n   registered before any action\" invariant for the worker half.\n2. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance -- the\n   governor never dispatches hardware and never itself handles the\n   animals).\n3. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op allowlist\n   is a HARD, PERMANENT block. This independently rejects any attempt\n   to slip in a breeding-finalization/animal-fitness/veterinary-\n   treatment/safety-officer-override-shaped op.\n4. **Farm basis** -- `:log-work-record`, `:schedule-crew-operation` and\n   `:coordinate-supply-order` must cite a REGISTERED AND VERIFIED farm\n   matching the worker's own farm (`:missing-farm-id` / `:unknown-farm`\n   / `:farm-unverified` / `:farm-mismatch`). `:flag-welfare-concern` is\n   deliberately EXEMPT from this check -- it is the channel by which a\n   brand-new farm, or an urgent welfare concern with no farm on file\n   yet, is surfaced for human intake, so it must not be blocked on the\n   very farm record it exists to help flag.\n5. **Work-record decision forbidden** -- `:log-work-record` is a\n   feeding-schedule/animal-condition-check-in metadata record ONLY.\n   Any proposal carrying an animal-treatment/welfare/breeding-decision\n   field (`husbandry.governor/log-record-forbidden-keys`:\n   `:treatment-decision`, `:breeding-decision`, `:welfare-disposition`,\n   `:fitness-for-breeding-decision`, `:veterinary-treatment-order`,\n   `:culling-decision`) is a HARD, PERMANENT block -- this actor never\n   records a treatment or breeding decision, only metadata check-ins.\n6. **Crew-schedule override forbidden** -- `:schedule-crew-operation`\n   is crew/task scheduling logistics ONLY. Any proposal carrying a\n   farm-safety-officer-judgment-override or a treatment/breeding-\n   directive field (`husbandry.governor/schedule-forbidden-keys`:\n   `:farm-safety-officer-override`, `:worker-safety-judgment-override`,\n   `:animal-handling-judgment-override`, `:treatment-directive`,\n   `:breeding-directive`, `:welfare-disposition`) is a HARD, PERMANENT\n   block -- this actor never overrides a farm safety officer's or\n   worker's real-time animal-handling judgment, it only schedules\n   which worker/crew is assigned to which task slot in advance.\n7. **Scope exclusion** -- independent, defense-in-depth layer on top of\n   checks 3/5/6: even for an otherwise-allowed op, any proposal whose\n   free text (`:rationale` or `:note`) names a finalization/execution\n   ACTION for an animal-treatment/welfare/breeding decision, or an\n   override of a farm safety officer's or worker's safety judgment\n   (`husbandry.governor/scope-excluded-terms`, e.g. \"finalize the\n   breeding decision\", \"determine the animal's fitness for breeding\",\n   \"order the veterinary treatment\", \"override the farm safety\n   officer's judgment\", plus Japanese equivalents) is a HARD, PERMANENT\n   block, evaluated unconditionally on content.\n\n### Two escalation gates, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-welfare-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's `always-escalate-ops`). This is the ONLY\n  channel by which the robot may surface an observation suggesting an\n  animal-welfare or injury-risk concern needs human attention -- it\n  never resolves the observation itself, and the robot's role ends at\n  \"here is the feeding/roster/condition-check-in status,\" never \"here\n  is whether the animal is fit for breeding\" or \"here is the treatment\n  decision.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-escalation-threshold` (800, inclusive boundary verified\n  by `ok-at-exact-supply-cost-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs\n  human sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION,\n  not a hard block: an over-budget supply order (e.g. a bulk feed\n  replenishment) is not itself unsafe, unlike an animal-treatment/\n  welfare/breeding or safety-officer-override attempt, which is why it\n  is handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate vs. structural\n  absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"breeding\", \"welfare\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path. This risk is especially\nsharp here because this actor's own op is literally named\n`:flag-welfare-concern` -- a bare \"welfare\" term would self-trip on\nevery single legitimate welfare-flag proposal.\n`husbandry.governor/scope-excluded-terms` is phrased exclusively as\nfinalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"finalize the breeding decision\",\n\"determine the animal's fitness for breeding\", \"override the farm\nsafety officer's judgment\" -- the exact phrasing pattern given in the\ntask's guidance on this known bug class), never bare nouns, and\n`husbandry.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-\ntrip-scope-exclusion` is a dedicated regression test asserting every\nop in the closed allowlist (including both the farm-present and\nfarm-nil variants of `:flag-welfare-concern`), driven through the\ndefault mock advisor, never trips `:scope-excluded` or\n`:op-not-allowed`. The mock advisor's own default rationale template\n(\"documented <op> for farm <id>\" / \"documented <op> (no farm yet --\nnew-farm intake)\") was written to avoid any bare-noun collision by\nconstruction. Green on first run this session -- the scope-exclusion\nself-trip guard itself was never tripped during development or\nverification.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"6129\" ...}` entry promoted\n  from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id` set to\n  the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6129` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-6129`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately before\n  the edit to account for concurrent sibling landings in this same\n  batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 45 tests, 138 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No implementation bug was found during this session's verification\n  pass -- the test suite was green on first run against both the local\n  working tree and the fresh post-push clone.\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- Full doc set published: README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\n  CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, SECURITY.md, LICENSE\n  (AGPL-3.0-or-later, byte-identical to the `cloud-itonami-isco-9332`\n  reference's LICENSE text), docs/business-model.md,\n  docs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-treatment-decision`/`:approve-\n  breeding-decision` that is merely gated behind `always-escalate-ops`\n  (the shape a naive reading of \"gate high-stakes actions behind human\n  approval\" might suggest): rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  guidance and the `ADR-2607152500` precedent -- an always-escalate op\n  still means the *capability* to finalize a treatment or breeding\n  decision exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human\n  gate. For decisions carrying both a physical-safety and an animal-\n  welfare dimension, the capability itself must not exist. The only op\n  related to escalating a concern is `:flag-welfare-concern`, which\n  surfaces the need for human attention and never itself decides,\n  authorizes, or defers a treatment/breeding action for later automatic\n  execution.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"breeding\" \"welfare\"\n  \"safety\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above -- this actor's own op is literally named\n  `:flag-welfare-concern`, so a bare \"welfare\" term would self-trip on\n  every legitimate welfare-flag proposal, and a bare \"breeding\" term\n  would self-trip on any legitimate check-in referencing breeding-\n  schedule metadata.\n- Merging `worker` and `farm` into a single entity (only checking one\n  provenance record): rejected -- the farm-mismatch check requires two\n  independently registered-AND-verified entities so that a farm record\n  a worker is not assigned to is caught even if both records\n  individually pass verification.\n- A numeric confidence-only gate for `:log-work-record` /\n  `:schedule-crew-operation` (no forbidden-keys content checks):\n  rejected as insufficient -- confidence measures the advisor's\n  certainty about the *proposal it chose to make*, not whether that\n  proposal smuggles forbidden content; a high-confidence advisor could\n  still misuse an allowed op's field shape to carry a forbidden\n  treatment or breeding decision, which is exactly why checks 5/6 exist\n  as independent, content-based, permanent HARD blocks regardless of\n  confidence.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- feeding-schedule/animal-condition-check-in\n   metadata only (checkin id, feeding-schedule status, animal\n   condition check-in, timestamp). Cannot carry a treatment or breeding\n   decision (HARD-blocked by check 5, `:work-record-decision-\n   forbidden`, on a closed forbidden-key list). Not an animal-\n   treatment/welfare/breeding decision in any respect -- it is a\n   records-management action indistinguishable in kind from a farm\n   clerk's log entry.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task scheduling logistics\n   only (task id, proposed time, crew id). Cannot carry a farm-safety-\n   officer-judgment override or a real-time animal-handling-judgment\n   override (HARD-blocked by check 6, `:crew-schedule-override-\n   forbidden`, on a closed forbidden-key list). Does not direct,\n   override, or draw any conclusion about a live animal-handling\n   decision -- it is a calendar action deciding in advance which\n   worker/crew is assigned to which task slot.\n3. **`:flag-welfare-concern`** -- surfaces an animal-welfare/injury-\n   risk concern to a human; ALWAYS escalates (governor's\n   `always-escalate-ops`, unconditionally, at every confidence level),\n   NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY channel through which\n   such a concern may be surfaced, and it explicitly never resolves,\n   decides, or acts on anything itself -- the human reviews and acts\n   entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- feed/supplies procurement proposal\n   (item, cost, vendor). Gated by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation\n   (above 800 always escalates). Not an animal-welfare or safety action\n   of any kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics action for feed/supplies\n   restocking.\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\nan animal-treatment/welfare/breeding decision, or an override of a\nfarm safety officer's or worker's safety judgment -- these categories\nare structurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed\nfour-op allowlist itself (`husbandry.governor/closed-op-allowlist`),\n(b) nine concretely-named forbidden-op fixtures that are rejected at\nboth the governor-unit level and the full-actor-graph level via a\nrogue advisor (`husbandry.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`husbandry.governor-test/every-scope-excluded-op-name-is-rejected`,\n`husbandry.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`),\n(c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`husbandry.governor/out-of-scope?`,\n`husbandry.governor/scope-excluded-terms`), tested end-to-end through a\nrogue advisor and the full compiled graph\n(`husbandry.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-rationale-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`),\nand (d) two additional content-shape guardrails preventing an\notherwise-allowed op from smuggling forbidden substance through its own\nfield shape (checks 5/6 above), both independently proven end-to-end\nvia a rogue advisor driving the full compiled StateGraph\n(`husbandry.actor-test/holds-a-treatment-decision-attempt`,\n`husbandry.actor-test/holds-a-crew-schedule-override-attempt`).\n\nThis actor coordinates FARM SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY -- it never\nhandles the animals and never makes a treatment/welfare/breeding\ndecision itself, matching the task's explicit domain-design\nconstraint.\n", :adr/date "2026-07-18", :actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 800}", :test/counts "{:total 45, :assertions 138, :failures 0, :errors 0}", :actor/coordination? true, :actor/name "Animal Producer Farm Scheduling and Logistics Coordination Practice", :db/id -1, :test/coverage [:governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :worker-verification :farm-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation :work-record-decision-forbidden :crew-schedule-override-forbidden :rogue-advisor-end-to-end], :registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-6129", :registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6129", :adr/scope [:kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6129], :test/simulator :husbandry.actor-test, :adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-6129 — Animal Producer Farm Scheduling and Logistics Coordination Coverage", :adr/source-format "edn", :adr/references [2607121000 2607152500], :actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-6129}]